    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This report examines the critical trade-off between accelerating fossil fuel phase-outs to meet urgent climate targets and the potential short-term economic volatility that higher energy prices may trigger. While rapid transitions promise long-term ecological security, they currently risk exposing vulnerable industries and households to acute financial instability before green infrastructure yields sufficient returns. The central tension lies not in whether climate action is necessary, but in precisely how governments can design transition pathways that deliver necessary emissions reductions without catastrophically disrupting essential economic sectors. Success will depend on implementing robust fiscal reforms and social safety nets to bridge the gap between immediate costs and future climate stability.",
      "source": "target",
